good evening it's an intriguing  prospects could the man who created the  world's biggest marketing and  advertising group now set up a rival to  take it on and will there be fresh  impetus to break up WPP following the  shock departure of Sir Martin Sorrell  well this is how WPP shares fur today  they closed down by nearly six and a  half percent well with me now it's  Johnny horn B of the advertising agency  the and partnership Johnny thanks very  much for joining me all for all the  speculation about what happens next to  WPP do you think this business is going  to get broken up I think what's  important now is that the new leadership  make a really good case and I think  there's a great case to be made for why  it should be together and I think you  know clearly Martin's passing as the end  of an era you know he is probably one of  the biggest figures in global in the  history of global media up there with  Rupert Murdoch and you know what he's  built was absolutely astounding and and  you know people will talk quite rightly  for a long time about his talents  equally the construct that he'd created  you can see it in the share price not  just today but over the last you know  year and a bit not just a WP but of all  the holding companies I think is telling  us that it needs to be constructed  differently and the speed with which  data and digital and technology have  come at the advertising industry  requires it to be reconfigured very  differently to the way that it's  configured at the moment now I think the  bit that you could be very optimistic  about if you are a believer in WPP is  that the two people have taken over I  think are in very very well positioned  for those changes Marc Reid is somebody  who has been at Martyn side since the  very beginning but I think crucially has  built all the digital assets within WPP  and digital is at the heart of what the  reconstruction needs to take place  around and Marc Reid came out of the  center working with Martin for the last  couple of years and took over one demand  one of the biggest pieces within WPP and  took that from I think a decline of six  or seven percent a year previously to  five percent year-on-year growth so I  think he's very well positioned I also  think Andrew Scott his other right-hand  man is somebody who knows this business  inside-out and there's been a lot of  speculation that says does martin run  all this from some magical black book  and  you know every asset in that business  Andrew Scott will know and understand so  I think there's a big challenge but I  couldn't think of two better people to  take it on so there they can be the glue  that Sir Martin was in terms of holding  all these disparate businesses together  yes and I think but I think also  crucially they need to take action to  reconfigure those businesses in a very  different way you know I think nowadays  to have the creative people the content  people divorce from the people who are  deploying that content in a world that's  now going very real time is increasingly  looking antiquated in all the holding  companies so they're going to have to  move fast to reconfigure it but they I  think they're two people that know that  and understand the digital world and  will move fast to do that because it's a  really interesting point you raised  there this is a business we had a global  growth at its strongest last year for  the first time since the financial  growth since the financial crisis W  peepees top-line organic revenues didn't  grow during the year I mean come on  earth suggests the business model is no  longer fit for the 21st century the  business model I mean I I passionately  believe that these things need to stay  together so there will be people who  will argue that the media should be sold  off separately all the creatives should  be sold off separately I think actually  the deployment of content needs to be  done hand in glove now with the creative  people almost in a real-time kind of  marketing newsroom environment I think  there are parts of WPP that you could  argue could be spun off you know the  research business to me wouldn't seem  something that is crucial anymore to the  big challenges the WPP faces and those  big challenges I think simply are at the  very top end you've got large  international businesses going through  business transformations trying to put  data at the heart of their organisations  and trying to put reconfigure their  businesses around personalization and  data there's new competitors now for the  holding companies with the likes of  Deloitte Accenture etc so at the top end  you need to reconfigure to make the case  I think and again I think Andrew and  Marc will do this for why the data and  the technology should be in service of  the ideas and the creativity not the  other way around and I think that's  where a WPP could win against against  those big consultants then you've also  got the challenge of the Facebook's and  the googles and you have to very well  make your case here  for why you need an honest transparent  broker on the media side you know as  long as we still have facebook making  claims as they did last year that there  are more teenagers on their platform  than there actually were teenagers in  the whole of the UK then I think there's  a role for somebody to say right we  should be between the brands and these  technology companies as the honest  brokers of media and I think if you can  make those two cases very well and  reconfigure the business then I think  you've got a very good chance of  bringing a background what about the  clients so I mean WP Pease got something  like 2 billion pounds worth of business  that comes up for tender this year yeah  a client's gonna walk  I don't think clients are going to walk  because of a change of leadership  despite the fact that Martin is you know  has been a fantastic leader I think  there is a case to be made that says  this change in this reconfiguration is  needed to happen for a couple of years I  mean personally I think Martin still had  the energy and the intellect and the  capability to have brought about that  you know brought about that change but  that change is now going to hand over to  to somebody else so I think those  clients won't walk if Mark and Andrew  are able to reconfigure these businesses  in a way that's incredibly relevant to  them and you know people of course have  personal relationships but business is  business dishes and decisions around  billion-pound  marketing campaigns will actually come  down to who has the best capabilities  and I think reconfigured at WPP is still  you know the man United of holding  companies it just needs to get its mojo  back go beefy Johnny you were named in  the weekend press as a possible  successor to Sir Martin do you have any  ambitions in that field no I think my  businesses is going along very nicely as  it is Ian and I and I have huge faith in  in in Andrew and and and Mark to do a  great job with it all right Johnny  who'll be great to see us ever thanks  for coming on

